Frozen input hashes matched. GGV PDF hash `8b426751…520c60`. Van den Essen is not in `refs/`; the locally verified statement is GGV (4.6) on PDF p. 17.

**What holds.** A Jacobian counterexample that minimizes `D = max(deg P, deg Q)` in its `Aut(C^2)×Aut(C^2)` orbit admits GGV’s standard subrectangular gauge with both degrees preserved. Van den Essen Cor. 10.2.21 supplies a source automorphism putting support in the rectangle `1 ≤ a ≤ b` with occupied northeast corner. If that map were nonlinear, GGV’s inverse-degree computation (unique top monomial `x^a y^b`, no cancellation) would give `deg P = v(R)(a+kb) > a+b = deg φ(P)`, so `φ` itself would lower `D`, contradicting minimality. Hence `φ` is affine, `nu` is preserved, and the top form is already `c x^u y^v` with `u,v ≥ 1`. So `nu(F_min) = 2`. Global `B` is not needed for this; orbit degree-minimality replaces it. Integration #14’s free-`E_0` reading and CH2 vacuity are then unconditional at every `N` in this scope.

A nonlinear automorphism *can* preserve `D` off the rectangle, by cancellation. It cannot when the image has a unique occupied NE corner with `a,b ≥ 1`. Source shears typically raise `D`; they drop `D` only by cancellation (the inverse of `(x, y+x^k)`). Target elementary maps drop `D` only in the `(LF)` divisibility case, which `(MIN)` already forbids at `F_min`.

**Wording repairs, not gaps.** (1) The degree-lowering map is `φ`, not `φ^{-1}`. (2) `nu = 2` uses the rectangle with `a,b ≥ 1`, not Definition 4.3’s `m,n > 1` and not global `B`. The short argument “linear change + (LF) monomializes any `H` with ≥ 2 roots” remains invalid: `GL(2)` cannot turn three roots into `x^u y^v`.

**Controls.** `(x, y+x^k)` is `nu=1` and not orbit-minimal (inverse drops `D` to 1). `(x,xy)` is `nu=2`; after the source shear `(x, y+x^2)` it becomes `nu=1` and `D: 2→3`. `x^2 y^3` after that shear has `D=8=2+3·2`. Three-root `xy(x-y)` stays `nu=3` under linear changes.

**AUDIT sentence.** OPEN[SUBRECT-ORBIT-BRIDGE] CLOSED YES: every Aut(C^2)×Aut(C^2) D-degree-minimal Jacobian counterexample admits a degree-preserving affine source change to GGV's standard subrectangular `(m,n)`-pair (van den Essen Cor. 10.2.21 as GGV (4.6), affinity by orbit D-minimality in place of global B), hence `nu(F_min)=2`, `E_0` is a free vertex of `T_+`, and CH2 is vacuous at every N in this scope; "linear change + (LF)" does not monomialize three or more roots.

